  "teamId": 134,
  "year": 2017,
  "narrative": "The 2017 Pittsburgh Pirates opened the season with a jolt of electricity, riding the bats of Starling Marte and Andrew McCutchen to early promise. Those two iconic Pirates combined for a pair of massive home runs in the first two weeks, and for a brief moment it felt like Pittsburgh might be a contender again. But the April record of 11-13 told a more complicated story — a team with talent but without the depth or consistency to string wins together.\n\nMay became a tale of two forces: clutch offensive moments and a bullpen in freefall. Jordy Mercer delivered one of the season's most electrifying moments with a walk-off single that plated three runs, and José Osuna and Mercer himself added timely home runs. Yet Tony Watson, once a reliable lefty specialist, became a symbol of the team's fragility, surrendering critical hits and home runs at the worst moments. The Pirates went 13-17 in May, and the gap between their ceiling and their floor was already painfully clear.\n\nJune was a wash in the standings at 13-13, but the narrative was defined by defensive lapses in the bullpen — Watson and Daniel Hudson repeatedly coughing up leads against Baltimore and Milwaukee. July brought a genuine respite, the team's best month at 14-11, fueled by timely hitting from Mercer, Adam Frazier, and Max Moroff. It was the closest Pittsburgh came to looking like a playoff team, a fleeting window of what might have been.\n\nAugust and September erased any lingering hope. The pitching staff — starters and relievers alike — gave up grand slams and crushing home runs, and the offense could not keep pace. Going 12-17 in August and 11-16 in September, the Pirates faded into irrelevance, finishing 75-87. The season became a quiet referendum on the franchise's direction, with McCutchen's declining production and a roster that lacked the arms to compete in a tough division. The final day's walk-off triple from Max Moroff was a bittersweet coda — a flash of youth on a team badly in need of reinvention.",
